      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step process to find the weight of one bowling ball. Here's the reasoning:\n\n1. **Identify the given information:**\n   - Seven identical bowling balls weigh the same as three identical canoes.\n   - Two canoes weigh a total of 56 pounds.\n\n2. **Determine the total weight of two canoes in terms of bowling balls' weights:**\n   Since the two canoes weigh 56 pounds together, we know that the total weight of all four bowling balls is also 56 pounds.\n\n3. **Determine the total weight of three canoes in terms of bowling balls' weights:**\n   Since one set of two canoes weight 56 pounds, then in three sets (which is the same as three canoes), the total weight is:\n   \\[\n   3 \\times 56 = 168 \\text{ pounds}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the weight of one canoe:**\n   Each canoe weighs one-third of the total weight of three canoes, since there are three canoes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{168 \\text{ pounds}}{3} = 56 \\text{ pounds}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the weight of one bowling ball:**\n   Now that we know two canoes weigh 56 pounds, the same total weight for the seven bowling balls means:\n   \\[\n   7 \\times \\text{(weight of one bowling ball)} = 56 \\text{ pounds}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\text{weight of one bowling ball} = \\frac{56 \\text{ pounds}}{7} = 8 \\text{ pounds}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe weight of one bowling ball is \\(\\boxed{8}\\) pounds.",
